22:27:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a really common question—and the idea that we taste sweet only at the tip and bitter only at the back is actually a widespread myth. It comes from an old diagram called the “tongue map,” which has been taught for decades but is scientifically inaccurate. Here’s the real story: **The myth’s origin** In 1901, a German scientist named D.P. Hänig measured how sensitive different parts of the tongue were to the four basic tastes (sweet, salty, sour, bitter). He found that the threshold for tasting each was slightly lower in certain areas—for example, the tip needed a bit less sweetness to detect it, and the back needed a bit less bitterness. Later, a Harvard psychologist named Edwin Boring misinterpreted Hänig’s graph as meaning those areas were the *only* places you could taste those flavors, and he turned it into the familiar tongue map in the 1940s. The map stuck in textbooks for years. **What we now know** All parts of the tongue (and even the soft palate, throat, and epiglottis) contain taste buds, and every taste bud can detect all five basic tastes: sweet, salty, sour, bitter, and umami. If you put a drop of bitter liquid on the tip of your tongue, you’ll still taste bitterness—the sensitivity might just be a tiny bit lower than at the back, but it’s very small and not noticeable in everyday eating. The differences are so minor that they don’t support a neat “zone” map. **Why the myth persists** It’s simple and easy to visualize, so it gets repeated. In reality, taste perception is much more complex—it involves smell, texture, temperature, and even the trigeminal nerve (which gives sensations like the coolness of mint or the heat of chili). So, to sum up: you don’t taste sweet only at the tip nor bitter only at the back. Your whole tongue is capable of tasting everything, and the old map is an oversimplification that science has left behind.
