22:29:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a fantastic analogy—and it cuts straight to the heart of why the tongue map debate can feel pedantic. You’re absolutely right that in science, we often say a region is “responsible for” something even when other regions contribute a little. So why is the tongue map considered a flat-out myth while the fovea’s role in color vision isn’t? The difference lies in the *scale* of specialization and the threshold for calling something a “myth” versus a “simplification.” Let’s compare the two cases carefully. ### The fovea and color vision - **Massive specialization**: The fovea packs almost all of the retina’s cones (the photoreceptors for detailed color vision) into a tiny 1.5 mm pit. Outside the fovea, cones drop off sharply; the periphery is overwhelmingly dominated by rods, which are color-blind in low light and give only weak, desaturated color in brighter conditions. - **Functional dominance**: If you aim your gaze directly at an object, you see its fine detail and vivid color. Try looking out of the corner of your eye—color and sharpness plummet. That’s because the fovea provides the brain with the vast majority of high-acuity chromatic information. The periphery can’t come close to replicating it, even for large stimuli. In everyday life, you *need* the fovea to have anything like normal color vision. - **The “responsible for” phrasing works**: Saying “the fovea is responsible for color vision” is a defensible shorthand, even though technically the peripheral cones do convey some color under photopic conditions. The fovea’s role is so overwhelming that neuroscientists often refer to it as *the* locus of photopic, high-acuity color vision. The simplified statement captures the essential truth, and the caveats are minor enough that it’s not called a myth. ### The tongue and taste regions - **Tiny sensitivity differences**, not functional dominance: Hänig’s original data showed that the tip might detect sweet at slightly lower concen