ee you feel cold perfectly well on your palm, so no one would draw a “cold map” with a cold-only fingertip zone. That’s what happened with the tongue: small threshold differences got blown up into an iconic, incorrect map. In short, the fovea’s dominance is so extreme that the simplified statement is basically true. The tongue’s regional differences are so subtle and non-exclusive that the simplified statement becomes false. The tongue map isn’t a “myth” because science hates loose language; it’s a myth because it took a whisper of a difference and shouted it as a rule, misleading millions of people about how their own bodies work. Your sour candy pucker is real, but it’s a whisper, not a rule.
